WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
 So now that we've carefully constructed

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.680
 our relationships in the tables,

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:07.740
 we need to reconstruct the data to show our users.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.000
 And you can kind of see how you would go

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.280
 pull this stuff together, but there's a wonderful capability

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.480
 in relational databases called JOIN

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.360
 that brings this all back together.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:20.680
 And so we have done this for efficiency of storage,

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.880
 efficiency of scanning, et cetera,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.520
 but we do need to traverse these foreign keys at times,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.520
 and the database software will do this for us automatically.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.160
 So the JOIN operation, basically,

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.400
 is a way to specify in a SELECT statement

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.360
 that you want to pull data out of more than one table,

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.080
 and then specifying using what's called the ON clause,

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.760
 exactly how you want that data pulled out.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.400
 And so here we go.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.400
 We already have a table, an album table to the artist table,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.400
 and the foreign key, and we want to, in effect,

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.280
 pull data from both the album and the artist,

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.560
 the album title and the artist name,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.160
 and we want to show that.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.200
 And so we're going to say SELECT,

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.800
 which is the same SELECT statement.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.240
 Here's a little different syntax.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.880
 This is the list of fields.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.440
 This is table.field, so it's the album title

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.380
 and the artist.name, comma, there, from the album.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.040
 And I always start with where the little arrow starts from,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.100
 album joined with.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.840
 So that is going to walk down this connection

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.400
 from album to artist, album joined with artist,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.780
 don't say with, I just say it, on,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.240
 and then this is the conditions

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.640
 upon which that join is going to happen.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.320
 When the album's artist ID, which is this column here,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.160
 album's artist ID matches, think of that as,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.280
 is equal to or matches the artist's ID.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.760
 And so it only connects the rows here

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.680
 when there is a match between these two tables.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.200
 And so if we look at this and we see that, you know,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.520
 this one matches this one and this one matches that one,

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.000
 and so it's the join connects conditionally

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.920
 and it connects when the on clause is satisfied.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.600
 And so when this whole join runs, this is what we get.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.320
 So you select all this stuff.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.520
 Now this is an abstraction.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.600
 Are you writing a loop?

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.480
 Are you doing two nested loops?

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.860
 How are you exactly bringing all this data together?

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.820
 We don't care about that because that's the beauty of SQL.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.320
 That's the beauty of how we do this in a database.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.420
 So now if we, we can just run this command.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.900
 So let's grab this command.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:32.160
 Select track title, genre name, from track, join genre,

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.000
 that exact query.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.440
 Case of keywords doesn't matter.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.240
 And we go over here and we run this as SQL.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:44.240
 And we run it, we get, oops, I got too far.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.560
 Let's do this one, so let's do that one there.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:52.860
 Select artist name.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.080
 I have to add that one to my little cheat sheet.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.720
 The next time you see the cheat sheet, it'll be right.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.600
 So the title, so this is coming from one table

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.540
 and that's coming from another table, okay?

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.380
 And so that's one.

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:12.160
 So here is something we can do

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:15.240
 that gives us a little more detail on that.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.520
 We can say, so this is where the connection,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.720
 and so you can think of the join as sort of spreading

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.520
 one table and connecting it to the other table.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:26.680
 And so what we're going to show here

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.040
 is it's exactly the same.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.320
 The other thing we're going to do

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:30.640
 is we're going to add these two columns

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.200
 so you can see where the match happens.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.840
 And so this is one table.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.320
 This is another table.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.540
 And these are the kind of columns in common,

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:41.380
 even though they're not.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:42.760
 They're the columns that match.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.280
 This is where the on clause is happening, right?

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:46.680
 We have taken

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:54.760
 this table joined with this table

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.740
 on these two things connecting with each other.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:00.400
 So you can almost, in some language,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.100
 some variants of SQL, this would even be a where clause.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.940
 So you connect these two rows,

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.800
 but only connect them when those two numbers match.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.800
 So you can see, I mean, if we run this,

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:12.840
 I'll just run this.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:22.760
 And again, you just see this is where it connects, okay?

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:27.760
 Now, interestingly, we can see what happens

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.080
 and what the purpose of the on clause is if we omit it.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.240
 So this is exactly the same as that previous query

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:37.960
 except there's no on clause.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.360
 So it's select all four of those fields

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:42.200
 from the track joined with the genre.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.320
 And so it's basically taken the track table

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.480
 and the genre with a join, but no on clause.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.440
 So it's not filtering for matches.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.880
 This is a match, this is a match,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.940
 that's a match, that's a match.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:57.440
 But we don't have an on clause,

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.440
 so the matchness doesn't matter.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.960
 And so you're going to get all possible combinations.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:06.080
 And literally, if there were 10 on one side

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.120
 and 30 on the other side,

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.840
 you would get 300 rows in that join.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:12.560
 So it'd be all combinations

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.920
 except the on clause reduces the combinations.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.320
 And you might think, whoa, this is really inefficient.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:19.720
 And I will say that's what my first reaction was

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.840
 when I first saw this, but it's not inefficient.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.360
 That's the beauty of abstraction,

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:25.480
 that's the beauty of SQL.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:29.120
 You say, do it, and it just figures that out.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:33.020
 So let me grab this and you will see

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:34.800
 that we can run this one as well.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.660
 And that kind of gives you why the on clause is important,

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:40.600
 because now we have a whole bunch of these things.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:43.300
 And the on clause just filters that out.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.440
 So if we would just add the on clause back in,

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:47.800
 then that would only show the ones

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:50.080
 we showed on the previous slide.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:51.640
 So that's why the on clause is important.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.040
 The join is like all possible combinations

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.240
 of all pairs of rows between these two tables.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.200
 On is, oh, but only where these two things match.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:01.920
 Now you might think that it's inefficient,

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.080
 but the on clause turns out to be the way it becomes efficient.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:11.120
 Okay.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:24.800
 So now we're going to do the same thing

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.000
 where we're just going to take the track title and the genre,

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.000
 going to connect that together.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.720
 So we select this, we need to join

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.800
 from one table, join to the genre table with an on clause.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.180
 And so we're going to make those connections.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:38.240
 And the other thing we're going to look at

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:40.820
 is the title and the genre name.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:47.880
 Oh, oops.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.000
 And then run that.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.880
 And so we got the title and genre name.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.680
 Now the thing you'll notice is for the first time,

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.200
 we now have replication of string data

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.800
 in a vertical dimension.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.360
 That's okay.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.000
 Because the data is not replicated in the database,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.080
 the data is now replicated as a result of the join.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.080
 And so we are going to reconstruct what the user wants to see,

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.160
 which the user originally, all the way back to the beginning,

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.800
 wanted to see the duplicate information

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.680
 in the vertical axis.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:20.640
 But now we're reconstructing it.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.320
 We didn't waste the space or performance in our database,

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:25.880
 but we still have to show them.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:30.040
 And so now the next thing we're going to do is a monster.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:34.160
 We are going to reconstruct across all four tables.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.760
 And you might think this is really hard,

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:39.160
 and sure it's going to be a little tricky,

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.040
 but as long as you follow the naming convention

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:42.680
 and the naming convention makes sense,

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:45.120
 we're going to do a select from the track's title,

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.440
 the artist's name, the album's title, and the genre name.

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:52.760
 From the track, join genre, join the album, join artist.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:55.760
 And so the joins follow the little arrows, right?

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:59.680
 And then the on clause qualifies each of those arrows

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.520
 when to follow the arrow.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:04.000
 And then this becomes pretty easy.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:04.880
 It's a foreign key.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:08.160
 The track's genre ID, that's a foreign key,

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.080
 equals genre.id.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:12.200
 The primary, that's primary key.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.720
 That's a foreign key because I name it that way.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.840
 And I know that this goes to that genre table

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:18.720
 because I name it that way.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.420
 And track's album ID is equal to the album's ID,

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.560
 foreign key, primary key.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.960
 And album's artist ID is equal to artist's ID.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:30.680
 After a while, you can type these pretty fast

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.800
 as long as you follow a naming convention

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:34.420
 and you know the naming convention.

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:36.800
 So this looks like it's really hard to do,

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.340
 but after a while, it's really just a pattern.

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:42.700
 So let's go ahead and run that one.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:49.540
 And it will, assuming we've done everything right,

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:50.600
 replicate all the data.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:52.400
 So there's all kinds of vertical data

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:53.360
 now being replicated.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.640
 Every column has vertical data.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.240
 Again, it's not in the database.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.040
 The select and the join are reconstructing

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:03.380
 vertical data as it needs to be shown to the user.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:08.320
 And so, if you've been following along,

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:10.540
 probably a couple hours later now,

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:12.560
 we started with a picture that was our mock-up

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:14.320
 of what we wanted our user interface to look like.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:15.480
 And it had vertical stuff.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:18.720
 And we're like, ah, we can't put that in a database model.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.120
 And then we carefully build a database model

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:22.080
 that didn't have the data.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.960
 And then we're like, ah, we got to reconstruct it.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:25.560
 So we use join to reconstruct it.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.800
 And so, after all that, we went here

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.840
 with a clean little model with four tables

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.520
 all beautifully connected together.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:35.020
 And then we had to join it all back together.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:36.760
 So join reconstructs it.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:40.400
 And again, the key is the storage is efficient,

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:43.660
 the scanning is efficient, and we still use the join

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:46.440
 to produce the output that we ultimately want

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.480
 with all the vertical replication

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.640
 that our users really want to see.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:56.400
 So, there's one more kind of relationship.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:58.640
 That was called a one-to-many relationship.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.920
 That was actually three one-to-many relationships.

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:02.640
 And the other major relationship

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:04.900
 is what's called a many-to-many relationship.

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:10.980
 (upbeat rock music)

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.480
 (upbeat rock music)

